<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- WHY CLEARTEXT IS PERMITTED APP-WIDE. A deliberate decision, recorded so it
     is not re-raised as a finding and not "fixed" by someone who does not know
     what depends on it.

     Every host the app talks to on its own behalf is HTTPS: TMDB, Simkl,
     MDBList, OMDb, OpenSubtitles, Supabase (auth + sync), Sentry and the
     GitHub update feed all use https:// endpoints and keep normal certificate
     validation. Nothing below weakens any of those; this file only declines to
     BLOCK plaintext for destinations the user chooses.

     Plaintext has to stay permitted because the interesting hosts are unknown
     until runtime and are frequently http:
       * IPTV playlist / XMLTV guide URLs, whose entry fields advertise
         "http://host/..." as the expected shape (see GuideScreen.kt).
       * Stremio-style addon manifests, also supplied by the user.
       * LAN pairing, where the phone-to-TV handoff is built as
         "http://<tv-ip>:<port>" (see AddonsScreen.kt) and a device on the
         local network generally has no certificate at all.
     Stream, poster and subtitle URLs from those sources are handled
     scheme-agnostically (StreamRanker.kt, KBRepository.kt,
     PlayerSourceExtras.kt), so blocking http would break playback for sources
     that work today, silently and only on the user's device.

     A domain allowlist cannot express this: this file is compiled into the
     APK, so it can only name hosts known at build time, while these are typed
     in by the user afterwards. Narrowing the exception to a few known domains
     would therefore change nothing for the sources that need it. The only
     real choice is permit cleartext or fail those connections.

     ACCEPTED RISK, stated plainly: for a user-added http source, the
     playlist/guide contents and any credentials embedded in them (an Xtream
     username/password carried in the URL, for instance) travel unencrypted, so
     someone on the same network could read or tamper with them. That exposure
     is confined to the sources the user themselves added; it does not extend
     to the app's own API traffic, which stays on HTTPS.

     TO TIGHTEN LATER: if plaintext sources are ever dropped, set
     cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" here, or delete this file and the
     android:networkSecurityConfig reference in AndroidManifest.xml. No other
     change is needed, because nothing in the app requires cleartext.
     (There is deliberately no android:usesCleartextTraffic attribute in the
     manifest: it is ignored on API 24+ whenever a network security config is
     present, and having both invites them to disagree.) -->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
